diff --git a/HISTORY.rst b/HISTORY.rst
index 18dc6e4a..2b0d036f 100644
--- a/HISTORY.rst
+++ b/HISTORY.rst
@@ -23,6 +23,8 @@ Fixed
 * DESeq2 and limma-voom differential expression (``differential_expression_deseq2``, ``differential_expression_limma_voom``, and their simplified modes) now check the requested comparisons, covariates, and likelihood-ratio-test factors against the design matrix before R is started. Previously a typo — such as a comparison of ``('condition', 'Ko', 'WT')`` when the design matrix says ``KO``, a trailing space, a misspelled factor or covariate, a non-numeric covariate, or a comparison between values of an all-numeric column (which R treats as a continuous variable rather than as groups) — was only discovered after R had run the entire model fit (which can take minutes), and then failed with an R error message that was hard to understand. Such mistakes now fail immediately with a plain-language error that names the problematic value and lists the valid alternatives. Valid inputs run exactly as before, and their results are unchanged.
 * DESeq2 and limma-voom differential expression no longer fail at the very end of the analysis when a design-matrix value contains a character that cannot be used in a file name. The names of the output tables are built from the factor and value names (for example ``DESeq2_condition_KO_vs_WT.csv``), so a value such as ``KO/1`` (a folder separator), ``10:30`` (illegal on Windows), or one containing a double quote previously made R fail to save the results after the whole model fit had run. **Output file names change for such values:** the characters ``< > : " / \ | ? *``, control characters, and line/paragraph separators are now replaced with ``_`` (so ``KO/1`` is saved as ``..._KO_1_vs_...``). All other characters — letters, digits, spaces, the remaining ASCII punctuation ``!#$%&'()+,-.;=@[]^_`{}~``, and any printable non-ASCII character such as ``ü`` or ``°`` — are kept, so names that were already valid are unchanged, including the established spelling of interaction terms in likelihood-ratio-test file names (``factor1_x_factor2`` for DESeq2, ``factor1Xfactor2`` for limma). If two different comparisons would end up with the same file name (for example the values ``KO/1`` and ``KO:1``, or values that differ only in upper/lower case, which Windows and macOS treat as the same file name), *RNAlysis* now reports this immediately instead of letting one table silently overwrite the other. Note that this check is applied on every operating system so that an analysis behaves the same everywhere: comparisons whose values differ only in case (such as ``KO`` and ``ko``) previously produced two files on Linux, and now need one of the values to be renamed. Values containing quotes or backslashes are now also passed to R safely, and limma-voom comparisons of values that R renames internally (such as ``KO-1``), or of factors whose names contain a dot (such as ``cell.type``), now work. Finally, the R scripts now read the count matrix without renaming its sample columns (R previously renamed e.g. ``24h-1`` to ``X24h.1`` internally) and verify inside R that the design matrix and the count matrix list the same samples in the same order. Results for all analyses that previously succeeded are unchanged (verified to be byte-identical on the test data).
 * GO enrichment now correctly rejects the ``'all.m'`` annotation-propagation method when it is combined with a statistical test other than Fisher's exact test, with a clear warning ("The 'all.m' propagation method is only compatible with Fisher's Exact test.") and an empty result, exactly as the ``'weight'`` method already did. ``'all.m'`` runs the ``'weight'`` algorithm internally, which only supports Fisher's exact test, but a typo in the check (``'allm'`` instead of ``'all.m'``) meant it never fired. As a result, single-set GO enrichment (XL-mHG) with ``'all.m'`` crashed with an uninformative ``TypeError``, and ``'all.m'`` with the hypergeometric or randomization test silently computed its ``'weight'`` component with Fisher's exact test instead of the chosen test. Runs that use ``'all.m'`` with Fisher's exact test (the default test) are unaffected and their results are unchanged. The same check also no longer ignores the general statistical-test checks: GO enrichment with an invalid statistical test, or single-set GO enrichment when the ``xlmhglite`` package is not installed, now stops with its warning and an empty result instead of carrying on to fail later. The documentation of the ``propagate_annotations`` parameter also now names the method ``'all.m'`` (the value you must actually pass) instead of ``'allm'``.
+* The help text (the "?" button and label tooltips) was silently missing from the GUI dialogs for about a hundred parameters, and now appears. The docstring reader that builds this help skipped any parameter description containing a character it did not expect (most often a semicolon, but also braces, ``|``, ``&`` and non-English characters), as well as the last parameter in a section, parameters listed after a blank line, and parameters whose line was accidentally indented. Affected parameters included ``power_transform`` on the PCA and clustering functions, ``non_unique_mode`` on the ortholog-mapping functions, ``propagate_annotations`` on GO enrichment and GO filtering, most options of ``plot_expression`` and ``scatter_sample_vs_sample``, and ``comparisons`` in limma-voom differential expression. Help text that was already displayed is unchanged. Missing help text was also written for ``go_ids`` (``filter_by_go_annotations``) and for ``mate_orientations``, ``allow_individual_alignment`` and ``allow_disconcordant_alignment`` (``bowtie2_align_paired_end``), and the help text of the ``organism`` parameter of ``filter_by_go_annotations`` and ``filter_by_kegg_annotations``, which used to be appended to the help of the ``mode`` parameter, now appears on its own.
+* Fixed ``bowtie2_align_paired_end`` with ``allow_disconcordant_alignment=False``: RNAlysis passed bowtie2 a misspelled option (``--no-discorcordant`` instead of ``--no-discordant``), so this setting did not work as intended. It now correctly tells bowtie2 not to report discordant alignments. Runs that kept the default (``allow_disconcordant_alignment=True``) are unaffected.
 
